@page "/tooling/inspector"

<PageTitle>WA Inspector</PageTitle>

<h1>WA Inspector</h1>
<p>
    WA Inspector is a browser extension for the UI review of pages that use Web Awesome, for example a Blazor UI that a
    coding agent built. You open the running page in Chrome or Edge, change the Web Awesome elements live, write comments,
    and export the change list with screenshots. The Claude Code plugin <code>wablazor</code> then applies the change list
    to the source code of the app.
</p>

<WaCallout Variant="WaVariant.Brand" IconName="circle-info">
    WA Inspector needs Chrome or Edge 116 or later, and the hand-off needs
    <a href="https://claude.com/claude-code">Claude Code</a>. The extension and the plugin are in the
    <a href="https://github.com/boldbrick/WebAwesome-Blazor">WebAwesome.Blazor repository</a>: the extension installs as
    an unpacked extension, and the plugin installs from the plugin marketplace in a clone of the repository.
</WaCallout>

<figure class="demo-figure">
    <img src="images/tooling/inspector-elements.png" width="1280" height="760"
         alt="The WA Inspector panel. On the left, the tree of the wa- elements of the Settings showcase. On the right, the details of the selected wa-input: its Blazor component and chain, the Apply to scopes and the attribute form with the changed appearance attribute." />
    <figcaption>The Elements tab: the tree of the page's Web Awesome elements and the forms of the selected element.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>What it does</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Shows the tree of the <code>wa-</code> elements and of the plain elements with Web Awesome utility classes.</li>
    <li>Changes attributes, CSS custom properties, CSS part styles, classes and inline style, and shows the result on the page at once.</li>
    <li>Changes the theme tokens (<code>--wa-*</code>) of the whole page.</li>
    <li>Adds a comment to any element.</li>
    <li>Exports the change list with screenshots for a coding agent.</li>
</ul>
<p>
    The inspector works on every page with Web Awesome elements. In a WebAwesome.Blazor app with the placement metadata on,
    it also knows which component and which source position rendered each element.
</p>

<h2>Install</h2>

<h3>1. Install the Claude Code plugin</h3>
<p>
    The plugin <code>wablazor</code> holds the developer skills of WebAwesome.Blazor: <code>install-inspector</code> and
    <code>wa-review-apply</code>. Clone the repository, then add its plugin marketplace and install the plugin once:
</p>
<pre><code>git clone https://github.com/boldbrick/WebAwesome-Blazor.git
claude plugin marketplace add &lt;clone folder&gt;/tools/wablazor-marketplace
claude plugin install wablazor@@wablazor-marketplace</code></pre>
<p>
    After an update of the clone, run <code>claude plugin marketplace update wablazor-marketplace</code> and
    <code>claude plugin update wablazor@@wablazor-marketplace</code>.
</p>

<h3>2. Install the browser extension</h3>
<p>On Windows, run this skill in Claude Code:</p>
<pre><code>/wablazor:install-inspector [Chrome | Edge | Both]</code></pre>
<p>
    A plugin from the marketplace of a clone does not bundle the extension, so the skill asks you for the extension folder:
    give <code>tools/wa-inspector/extension</code> of your clone. The skill copies the extension to
    <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\WABlazor\wa-inspector-extension</code>, opens Chrome or Edge, puts the folder path on the clipboard
    and tells you the steps on the extensions page. Only you can load an unpacked extension into the browser. Run the skill
    again after an update to update the extension.
</p>
<p>To install manually, for example on macOS or Linux, where the skill does not run:</p>
<ol>
    <li>Open <code>chrome://extensions</code> (Chrome) or <code>edge://extensions</code> (Edge).</li>
    <li>Turn on <strong>Developer mode</strong>.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Load unpacked</strong>.</li>
    <li>Select the folder <code>tools/wa-inspector/extension</code> of your clone, or the folder that the skill made.</li>
    <li>Reload the pages that were open before. The extension loads with the page.</li>
</ol>
<p>
    After an update of the extension files, click the reload button of the extension on the extensions page, and then reload
    the inspected page.
</p>

<h2>Turn on the Blazor metadata</h2>
<p>
    With <code>WebAwesomeOptions.EmitInspectorMetadata</code> on, the root element of every wrapper gets
    <code>data-wablazor-*</code> attributes: the wrapper type, the chain of the application components from the render root,
    and two hashes of its source position. The inspector then groups the instances of one source position, offers the scope
    <strong>Same source site</strong>, and the coding agent finds the source more easily. Without the metadata the
    inspector still works, but the locators are less exact.
</p>
<p>Use one of these settings:</p>
<ul>
    <li>the option <code>EmitInspectorMetadata = true</code> in the <code>AddWebAwesome</code> delegate;</li>
    <li>the configuration key <code>WebAwesome:EmitInspectorMetadata</code> set to <code>true</code>, when the app calls <code>AddWebAwesome(builder.Configuration)</code>;</li>
    <li>the environment variable <code>WebAwesome__EmitInspectorMetadata=true</code>, with the same call.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>builder.Services.AddWebAwesome(options =>
    options.EmitInspectorMetadata = builder.Environment.IsDevelopment());</code></pre>
<WaCallout Variant="WaVariant.Warning" IconName="triangle-exclamation">
    Turn on the metadata in development and test environments only. The attributes show the names of the application's
    components, and the metadata costs some render time.
</WaCallout>
<p>
    The setting is process-wide. In a Blazor Web App with WebAssembly or Auto render modes, the client project calls
    <code>AddWebAwesome</code> too: set the option there also, for example in <code>wwwroot/appsettings.Development.json</code>.
    The default is <code>false</code>.
</p>

<h2>Review a page</h2>

<h3>Open the inspector</h3>
<p>One panel runs in three places:</p>
<table class="demo-table">
    <thead>
        <tr><th>Mode</th><th>How to open it</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td>DevTools panel</td>
            <td>Open DevTools (F12), then the <strong>Web Awesome</strong> tab</td>
            <td><strong>Follow $0</strong> selects the element that the Elements panel selects. <strong>Inspect in Elements</strong> shows the selected element there.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Side panel</td>
            <td>Click the <strong>WA Inspector</strong> button in the browser toolbar</td>
            <td>Follows the active tab of its window</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Tab</td>
            <td>Open <code>chrome-extension://&lt;extension id&gt;/panel.html?tabId=&lt;tab id&gt;</code></td>
            <td>Inspects one tab</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
<p>The status line under the toolbar shows the page title, the Web Awesome version and whether the page has Blazor placement metadata.</p>

<h3>Select an element and a scope</h3>
<p>
    Click an element in the tree (the filter box finds a tag, a class, a text or a component), or click <strong>Pick</strong>
    and then the element on the page. The details show the tag, the text, the ancestors, and the Blazor component and chain.
    <code>×3</code> in the tree means that one source position renders three instances.
</p>
<p>Under <strong>Apply to</strong>, select which elements a change is for:</p>
<table class="demo-table">
    <thead>
        <tr><th>Scope</th><th>Use it when</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>This placement</td><td>The change is for all instances of this position in the source, for example all rows of one list</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Same source site</td><td>The change is for this markup line wherever its component is used (Blazor metadata only)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>All of this tag</td><td>The change is for every element with this tag, on every page</td></tr>
        <tr><td>This instance</td><td>The change is for one element only</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
<p>
    The first change makes a numbered item with a color. More changes to the same element in the same scope go to the same
    item. <strong>Add marker</strong> makes an item without a change, for a comment.
</p>

<h3>Change the element</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Attributes</strong>: select or type a value, or set a check box. The hint shows the page's own value.</li>
    <li><strong>CSS custom properties</strong>: type a value. The placeholder shows the default.</li>
    <li><strong>Part styles</strong>: select a part, then type CSS declarations, for example <code>padding: 0; gap: 4px</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Classes</strong>: remove a class, or add one. The list proposes the Web Awesome utility classes, for example <code>wa-gap-l</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Style</strong>: type CSS declarations for the element.</li>
    <li><strong>Comment</strong>: write what must change and why. The coding agent reads the comment as the intent, so write the intent, not only the value. A comment can be the only content of an item, also on a plain element.</li>
</ul>
<p>
    When the instances of one placement have different values, the attribute is data-driven. The panel then offers a
    <strong>remap</strong> instead of one value: for example, <code>warning</code> becomes <code>danger</code> and
    <code>neutral</code> stays.
</p>
<p>
    The page shows each change at once. The changes stay while you navigate and after a reload, until you delete the item.
    Set <strong>Markers</strong> on to see the numbered outlines of the items on the page.
</p>

<figure class="demo-figure">
    <img src="images/tooling/inspector-page-markers.png" width="1280" height="760"
         alt="The Settings showcase with three review items applied: numbered, colored outlines around the Workspace name input, the density button group and the auto-save switch, and the brand color changed by a theme token." />
    <figcaption>The reviewed page with the numbered markers of the items and a changed brand color.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3>Change the theme</h3>
<p>
    On the <strong>Theme</strong> tab, filter the tokens (for example <code>color-brand</code> or <code>space</code>) and type a
    new value, or select a color for a <code>--wa-color-*</code> token. A theme change applies to the whole page.
</p>

<figure class="demo-figure">
    <img src="images/tooling/inspector-theme.png" width="1280" height="435"
         alt="The Theme tab of the WA Inspector panel, filtered to color-brand, with a changed value of the brand fill token." />
    <figcaption>The Theme tab with a changed brand token.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3>Check the change list</h3>
<p>The <strong>Changes</strong> tab lists all items with their number, color, target, scope, changes and comment, and a status:</p>
<table class="demo-table">
    <thead>
        <tr><th>Status</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>pending</code></td><td>The page does not have the values on its own yet</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>applied</code></td><td>The page has the values on its own: the code has the change</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>unchecked</code></td><td>The item has no attribute or class change that the inspector can compare (CSS, parts, style, remaps, comments)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>not on this page</code></td><td>No element on this page matches the item</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<figure class="demo-figure">
    <img src="images/tooling/inspector-changes.png" width="1280" height="359"
         alt="The Changes tab of the WA Inspector panel with three numbered items: an attribute change on wa-input, a comment on the density buttons and an attribute change with a comment on wa-switch, each with its status." />
    <figcaption>The Changes tab: the items of the review with their status.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3>Export</h3>
<p>
    Click <strong>Export</strong>. The inspector writes a folder <code>Downloads/wa-review/&lt;yyyy-MM-dd_HHmmss&gt;/</code> with:
</p>
<ul>
    <li><code>changes.json</code>: the machine-readable change list (schema <code>wa-inspector/change-list@@1</code>);</li>
    <li><code>changes.md</code>: the same change list for people;</li>
    <li><code>page.png</code>: a screenshot of the visible page with the numbered outlines;</li>
    <li><code>item-NN.png</code>: one screenshot for each item that matches an element on the page.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Copy</strong> copies the change list as Markdown, without screenshots, for example for a ticket.</p>

<h2>Hand off to Claude Code</h2>
<p>Open Claude Code in the repository of the reviewed app and run the skill with the export folder:</p>
<pre><code>/wablazor:wa-review-apply Downloads/wa-review/2026-10-20_143012</code></pre>
<p>
    The skill reads <code>changes.json</code> and looks at the screenshots. The reviewer's comments come first: the values show
    the intent, they are not a patch. The agent then:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>finds the source through the component chain, the text, the classes and the CSS isolation scope of each element;</li>
    <li>makes the change at the narrowest place that gives the chosen scope: one line for a placement, the shared component for a source site, an app-wide rule for a tag, and a rule from the data for one instance;</li>
    <li>writes attributes as wrapper parameters (<code>with-caret</code> becomes <code>WithCaret</code>, closed value sets become enum members), and changes the mapping code for a remap of a data-driven value;</li>
    <li>puts CSS custom properties and part styles into the app CSS, utility classes into layout wrapper parameters or <code>Class</code>, and theme tokens into the app's theme CSS;</li>
    <li>solves the cases that the inspector cannot show (hover and focus states, other breakpoints, the dark theme), as the intent requires;</li>
    <li>builds the app, runs the tests of the changed components, and reports each item as done, changed differently (and why) or not done (with a question).</li>
</ul>
<p>
    The skill also works for apps with plain Web Awesome HTML. Restart or reload the app, then reload the page with the
    inspector open: the items whose attribute and class changes are now in the code show <code>applied</code>.
    <strong>Remove applied items</strong> removes them. Check the <code>unchecked</code> items on the page, then delete them.
</p>

<h2>Limits</h2>
<ul>
    <li>A data-driven value that is the same on all instances on the current page looks like a literal value.</li>
    <li>One instance of a loop cannot get its own literal value. Use a comment or a value remap.</li>
    <li>Property-only settings (no attribute) and slot content are read-only. Write a comment.</li>
    <li><code>wa-</code> elements inside the shadow root of another <code>wa-</code> element are shown as internal and are not edited.</li>
    <li>Placements are stable for one build. A change of the Razor markup can change them. Old exports stay readable, because the locator also has the chain, the classes and a text snippet.</li>
    <li>The skill <code>install-inspector</code> runs on Windows only. On other systems, load the extension manually.</li>
</ul>

